  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Crepidotus crocophyllus, commonly known as Saffron Crep, is a small shelf or bracket fungus characterized by its distinctive saffron-yellow to orange gills. This delicate wood-decay fungus is inedible and belongs to the Crepidotaceae family, found throughout hardwood forests in New England.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Saffron-colored gills",
      "description": "The gills are distinctively bright saffron-yellow to orange, becoming rusty-brown with age as spores mature",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Small, shelf-like fruiting bodies",
      "description": "Fruiting bodies are typically small (1-3 cm wide), fan or shell-shaped, and grow from wood in overlapping clusters",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "White to pale cap surface",
      "description": "The cap is small, convex, and covered in fine hairs, ranging from white to cream-colored with a small or absent stem",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Saffron Crep grows on decaying hardwood, particularly on fallen logs, branches, and dead trees in moist New England forests throughout spring and fall.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The saffron-yellow color of the gills gives this fungus its common name and is one of the most visually striking features among small wood-decay fungi in the region.",
